= Chapter One: The Ash and the Ink

The bell of the Seventh Spire tolled three times, its deep bronze resonance vibrating through the timber beams of the old archives. Outside the leaded glass windows, rain fell in silver sheets over the slate rooftops of the lower ward.

Kaelen dipped his quill into the dark iron gall ink, his fingers stained purple from three days of continuous copying. On the vellum before him lay the fragments of the Old Imperial Treaty—words written three centuries ago by men who had long turned to dust.

"You should not be reading those passages, boy," Master Vance's voice broke the rhythm of the rain. The old archivist stood by the threshold, a sputtering tallow candle trembling in his gnarled hand.

"They aren't just treaties, Master," Kaelen replied quietly without lifting the nib from the page. "They are warnings."

Vance stepped forward, the floorboards groaning under his weight. His face was drawn tight, shadows pooling beneath his hollow cheeks. "Some truths are buried because the world cannot bear their weight. Close the folio. Pack your things."

#scene-break()

Before Kaelen could ask why, a sudden crash echoed from the street below—the splintering sound of heavy oak gates giving way under iron hammers.

He rushed to the narrow window. Through the veil of rain, torches flickered in the courtyard like malicious fireflies. Armed men in dark iron mail were pouring through the breach, their shields emblazoned with the silver raptor of the Grand Inquisitor.

"They are already here," Vance said softly, his voice devoid of panic, replaced only by a dreadful, resigned stillness. "The river ferry departs at the turn of the hour. Take the scroll in the iron cylinder. Do not let them seize it."

= Chapter Two: The Crossing

The river was a swollen black ribbon beneath the midnight fog. Behind them, the spires of the city burned with a pale, unnatural fire, smoke blotting out the constellations.

"Keep your head down," Vance whispered, tossing a rough wool cloak over Kaelen's shoulders. The ferryman took the silver coin without a word, pushing the heavy wooden barge into the current.

Kaelen clutched the leather cylinder to his chest. Inside was the single scroll Vance had risked his life to extract before the archives fell. Every instinct screamed at Kaelen to turn back, to return to the only life he had ever known. But as the dark shoreline receded into the mist, he knew that world was gone forever.

#scene-break()

The cold water lapped against the hull with a steady, hypnotic rhythm. Across the river, the dark silhouettes of the Whispering Pines rose against the sky, ancient and indifferent to the fall of empires.
